  "summary": "Recent preprints apply tensor, latent-variable and digital twin models to align heterogeneous neural data across sessions, subjects and recording formats.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread in these preprints is the extraction of shared latent structure from neural data that differ in session, subject or recording format. Neuralign jointly aligns and decomposes heterogeneous recordings into shared tensor components, while Unified CEBRA pools recordings across sessions through a shared label to relate single-neuron contributions to population codes in mouse visual cortex. C3PO operates directly on unsorted, unbinned continuous-time spike trains, learning predictive and interpretable latent variables. A digital twin of freely moving mouse V1 links classical tuning and behavioral gain effects to a general visual code. In Drosophila, heterogeneous short-term plasticity across Kenyon cell inputs is reported to reformat temporal information into an explicit population clock. The items do not address identical questions; the shared emphasis is on recovering interpretable latent variables while treating heterogeneity as part of the structure to be modeled.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
